# Garbage collection in one breath

## Garbage = unreachable

From **GC roots** (stack locals, statics), the collector follows every reference. Reachable = live; the rest is garbage.

```java
var user = new User("Ana");   // reachable
user = null;                  // now unreachable → collectible
```

Reachability, **not** "will I use it" — a held reference you never touch stays alive. That gap causes most leaks.

## Generational hypothesis — most objects die young

- **Young gen** — new objects; **minor GC** is small, frequent, fast
- Survivors are **promoted** to the **old gen** — collected rarely by a **major GC**

## Collectors (pick with a flag)

**G1** (default) · **Parallel** (throughput) · **ZGC / Shenandoah** (low-pause, concurrent). Tradeoff is the **stop-the-world pause**.

## What you do

- **Don't** call `System.gc()` — a hint; forces a costly full GC
- **Do** avoid leaks — unintended refs: growing `static` collections, caches, listeners
- One knob: `-Xmx` (max heap)
